== Project Charter

A Project Charter is a formal document that officially starts and authorizes a project. It gives the project manager the authority to use organizational resources for project activities, connects the project with the organization's goals and objectives, and is usually prepared by the project sponsor or senior management to act as the foundation of the project.

=== Contents of a Project Charter
+ *Project Title and Description:* Includes the project name and a brief description.
+ *Project Purpose and Objectives:* Explains why the project is being done and its goals.
+ *Project Scope (High-Level):* Defines project boundaries, deliverables, and expected outcomes.
+ *High-Level Requirements:* Describes the major requirements needed for the project.
+ *Project Manager and Authority:* Identifies the project manager and their level of authority.
+ *Project Sponsor:* Identifies the person or department that approves and funds the project.
+ *Key Stakeholders:* Lists important people or groups involved in the project.
+ *High-Level Risks and Assumptions:* Identifies possible risks, limitations, and assumptions.
+ *Milestones and Timeline:* Provides important project phases and expected completion dates.
+ *Budget Summary:* Provides an estimated project budget and financial resources.
+ *Approval Section / Signature:* Contains signatures of the sponsor and project manager for approval.


== Developing Project Charter (PMI)

The Project Charter Development process is performed during the Initiating Process Group. It formally authorizes the project.

===  Inputs
+ *Business Documents:* Includes business case and benefits management plan.
+ *Agreements:* Includes contracts, MOUs, and service agreements.
+ *Enterprise Environmental Factors (EEF):* Includes company culture, government rules, market conditions, and external factors.
+ *Organizational Process Assets (OPA):* Includes templates, previous project records, and lessons learned.
+ *Statement of Work (SOW):* Defines project work, objectives, scope, deliverables, and timelines.

=== Tools and Techniques
+ *Expert Judgment:* Uses knowledge and experience of experts, consultants, and managers.
+ *Data Gathering:* Collects information through brainstorming, interviews, and focus groups.
+ *Interpersonal and Team Skills:* Includes communication, conflict management, and meeting skills.
+ *Meetings:* Discussions with sponsors, stakeholders, and management to prepare the charter.

===Outputs
+ *Project Charter:* The main output that officially authorizes the project.
+ *Assumption Log:* A document that records project assumptions and constraints.

== SWOT Analysis 

SWOT analysis is a strategic planning tool used to identify and evaluate the Strengths, Weaknesses, Opportunities, and Threats of an individual, project, or organization. It helps managers understand internal and external factors that can affect the success of a project or business.

+ *Strengths (S):* Internal positive factors that give an organization an advantage (*Example:* Good reputation, skilled workforce, strong financial position).
+ *Weaknesses (W):* Internal negative factors that limit performance or create disadvantages (*Example:* Lack of expertise, limited resources, poor technology adoption).
+ *Opportunities (O):* External favorable factors that an organization can exploit to grow or improve (*Example:* Growing market demand, new technology, government support).
+ *Threats (T):* External negative factors that may create risks or challenges (*Example:* Strong competition, changing customer preferences, economic downturns).

*Purpose of SWOT Analysis:*
- To match strengths with opportunities.
- To reduce weaknesses and defend against threats.
- To support strategic decision-making.

=== Example: Software Development Startup 
- *Strengths:* Skilled developers, good programming knowledge, creative ideas.
- *Weaknesses:* Limited budget, small team, lack of marketing experience.
- *Opportunities:* Growing demand for software applications, new technology, online business growth.
- *Threats:* High competition, rapid technology changes, large established companies.

== Statement of Work (SOW) 

A Statement of Work (SOW) is a formal document that defines the scope, objectives, deliverables, and timeline of a project. It describes what work will be done, who will do it, how it will be done, and when it will be completed. SOW is usually prepared during the planning phase and agreed upon by the client and service provider before starting the project to prevent misunderstandings.

* Main Contents of SOW*

+ *Introduction / Purpose:* Explains why the project is being done.
+ *Scope of Work:* Defines the boundaries of the project (what is included and excluded).
+ *Objectives / Goals:* Describes the expected outcomes of the project.
+ *Deliverables:* Specifies the outputs produced by the project (e.g., reports, software, hardware).
+ *Schedule / Timeline:* Defines project deadlines and important milestones.
+ *Roles and Responsibilities:* Defines who is responsible for each task.
+ *Standards & Acceptance Criteria:* Defines how deliverables will be measured and approved.
+ *Cost / Budget:* Provides estimated project expenses.

